---
import { getCollection } from "astro:content";
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import { publishedOnly } from "../lib/record.ts";

const posts = publishedOnly(await getCollection("posts"));
const sourceCount = new Set(
	posts.flatMap((p) =>
		p.data.sources.map((s) => {
			try {
				return new URL(s).hostname.replace(/^www\./, "");
			} catch {
				return s;
			}
		}),
	),
).size;
---

<Base
	title="About — Chino Valley Today"
	description="How Chino Valley Today is made: primary sources, automated writing and checks, human review of sensitive items, and visible corrections."
>
	<header class="abouthead">
		<div class="shell abouthead__inner">
			<h1 class="abouthead__title">
				A record of what local government did, with the receipts attached.
			</h1>
		</div>
	</header>

	<div class="reading body prose">
		<p>
			Chino Valley Today covers the meetings that decide things in Chino and
			Chino Hills &mdash; the two city councils, the CVUSD Board of Education,
			and the commissions that rule on development. It is built for two
			readers at once: someone who cannot sit through a three-hour weeknight
			meeting, and someone who needs to check exactly what was decided.
		</p>

		<h2>Every claim carries its source</h2>
		<p>
			The first editorial rule is the shortest one: <strong
				>no source, no claim</strong
			>. Each entry links to the primary record it came from &mdash; an agenda
			item, a staff report page, a video timestamp, a state licence report, an
			agency alert. The
			<span class="stamp-inline">violet stamps</span> throughout this site mark those
			sourced claims, and inside an entry every one is a link you can follow. Violet means a
			primary record and nothing else: reporting from other outlets is linked
			in outline blue and labelled as attribution, so the two are never
			mistaken for each other.
		</p>
		<p>
			This is a record of the record. It reports what a public body did. It
			does not characterise motives, tone, or sides beyond what was actually
			said on the record.
		</p>

		<h2>What is checked before anything publishes</h2>
		<p>
			Most of this site is produced by software, and most entries publish
			without a person reading them first. Entries are assembled from public
			documents and then routed by how much judgment they need.
		</p>
		<ul>
			<li>
				<strong>Tier A</strong> is rendered directly from structured data with
				no language model involved, and publishes automatically. A template
				that quotes verbatim and links out cannot invent anything.
			</li>
			<li>
				<strong>Tier B</strong> is written by an AI language model from the
				source documents. It publishes automatically, with no person reading
				it first, but only after two checks: an automated pass
				that verifies every paragraph carries a citation, that every number and
				date appears in the source material, and that every proper name is one
				the sources actually used &mdash; and then a review by a second AI
				model from the same family, which can send a post back for an
				automated rewrite or hold it, never clear one another check has
				flagged. About one in eight of the entries published this way is
				flagged for a person to check afterwards.
			</li>
			<li>
				<strong>Tier C</strong> is held for a person to read and clear by
				hand: crime items naming a private individual, anything involving a
				minor, and anything touching allegations. There is one exception.
				Alerts from the Sheriff's notification channels publish automatically
				as the agency's own headline and a link to its page, never the text of
				the release. A headline can name a person. An alert that indicates a
				minor is involved is still held.
			</li>
		</ul>
		<p>
			The weekly podcast is written by an AI model from published entries and
			read by synthetic voices. When an episode is held for review, a person
			clears the script, not the finished audio.
		</p>
		<p>
			Anything that fails any check is held rather than published. A missed
			week costs little; a wrong entry naming a resident costs trust that does
			not come back. AI models still make mistakes these checks can miss,
			which is why every claim links to its source and corrections are
			visible.
		</p>

		<h2>Corrections</h2>
		<p>
			Corrections are visible. A corrected entry keeps the original text struck
			through with a dated note attached &mdash; never a silent edit. Where
			leaving the original visible would cause harm, such as a name that
			should not have been published, the text is removed and the note says
			so.
		</p>
		<p>
			To report an error, email <a href="mailto:corrections@chinovalley.today"
				>corrections@chinovalley.today</a
			>. Quote the entry and, where you can, point to the record that shows what
			the correct version is &mdash; the same standard this site holds itself
			to. A correction to a Tier B entry also counts against the checks that let
			it through. Two in a rolling month flag that kind of entry, and the
			editor then decides whether to stop it publishing automatically until
			the checks are tightened.
		</p>

		<h2>Where the material comes from</h2>
		<p>
			Sources are the agencies' own: council and board agenda systems, meeting
			video and its captions, state licence reports, and agency-operated
			notification channels ingested the way those channels intend to be read.
			Other publications are linked by headline with a short excerpt of their
			own description, cut to length by software, never the article itself. Social platforms are not used as sources.
		</p>
		<p class="stat mark">
			{posts.length} entries published &middot; {sourceCount} source
			{sourceCount === 1 ? " authority" : " authorities"} cited
		</p>
	</div>
</Base>

<style>
	.abouthead {
		background: var(--crate);
		color: #fff;
		border-bottom: 6px solid var(--placard);
	}
	.abouthead__inner {
		padding-block: 1.9rem 2.2rem;
		max-width: 44rem;
	}
	.abouthead__title {
		margin: 0;
		font-size: clamp(1.55rem, 1.2rem + 2vw, 2.5rem);
		line-height: 1.16;
		font-weight: 600;
	}
	.body {
		padding-block: 2.4rem 1rem;
	}
	.stamp-inline {
		color: var(--stamp);
		font-weight: 600;
	}
	.stat {
		margin-top: 2.4rem;
		padding-top: 1rem;
		border-top: 1px solid var(--galv-edge);
		font-size: 0.72rem;
		letter-spacing: 0.1em;
		color: var(--muted);
	}
</style>
